> ```
> We assume discrete time, $t = 0, \ldots, T$, with time steps of $\Delta t$ (our main results assume $\Delta t = $ 1 hour, and $T$ = 36). At time $t$, each EV $i$ has a state of charge $s_{it} \in [0, 1]$, which is not known with certainty at time $t-1$.
> 
> An individual EV solution consists of a real-time management function $f_i\left(t, s_{it}\right)$ which maps observed state of charge (SOC) to a charging decision. Charging decisions are denoted $q_{it}$, in units of kW, with positive values for charging and negative values for discharging. EV owners have an expected driving schedule, simplified to drive at $t_d$ and park at $t_p$ below, but may deviate from it.
> 
> The equation of motion for this system is
> \[
> s_{it} = \begin{cases}
>     s_{i,t-1} + q_{it} \Delta t / C_i & \text{if $d_{it} = 0$} \\
>     s_{i,t-1} - \Delta s & \text{if $s_{i,t-1} - \Delta s_{it} > s_{it}^\text{MIN}$} \\
>     s_{it}^{MIN} & \text{otherwise}
> \end{cases}
> \]
> where $C_i$ is the EV battery capacity, $\Delta s_{it}$ is a decline in the state of charge per timestep when driving, and $s_{it}^\text{MIN}$ is the minimum allowed state of charge.
> 
> In our analysis, we assume all cars are identical, and driver conditions are time-invariant, so $C_i = C$, $\Delta s_{it} = \Delta s$, and $s_{it}^\text{MIN} = s^\text{MIN}$. We maintain the $i$ and $t$ indices in this section for generality.
> 
> \subsection{Objective functions}
> 
> Multiple EVs can be aggregated to represent a single power provider to the grid (an ``aggregator''), and to offer grid services. An aggregator solution consists of the collection of management functions for its contributing EVs and a series of regulation services bids $r_t$, provided 24-hours in advance. The management functions only apply when the EV is plugged into a managed charging station. We define $M_{mt}$ as the set of EVs plgged into managed charging stations under Monte Carlo $m$ and time $t$.
> 
> The realized behavior of the EV owners consists of actual periods of use of the EV, $d_{it} = 1 \;\text{if $i \notin M_{it}$}$ and the SOC level of the EVs upon plugging back in, $s_{it}$ if $d_{i,t-1} = 1$. These are represented as a Monte Carlo, $\{ d_{mit}, s_{mis} \text{ for } s \in \{ t | d_{i,t-1} = 1 \} \}$. The real-time management function can then extend the SOC timeseries to all times, $s_{mit}$.
> 
> The evaluation criteria for an individual EV solution is a function $g_i\left(s_{mit}, q_{mit} \right)$. This function combines individual profit (mainly based on $q_{mit}$) with other utility (mainly based on $s_{mit}$). The total score is the expected value of this function across Monte Carlo draws. It is only evaluated during periods when the car is plugged in at a managed charging station.
> 
> The total evaluation of the aggregator behavior combines the sum of EV evaluations with an evaluation of the realized provision of regulatory services. That is,
> \begin{equation}
> \mathbb{E}_m \left[h\left(r_t, \{q^\text{REGMAX}_{mit}\}, \{q_{mit}\} \right) + \sum_{i \in M_{mt}} g_i\left(s_{mit}, q_{mit} \right)\right]
> \label{eqn:objective}
> \end{equation}
> 
> The $q^\text{REGMAX}_{mit}$ is the maximum regulation charging allowed for a given EV, accounting for the charging rate, and the constraints that the SOC never exceeds the SOC bounds in the service of regulation services.  Total regulation services can be distributed across EVs dynamically. Since the regulator guarantees that charging will be energy neutral over a period of $\tau$, this latter constraint conservatively modeled as a continuous charging (discharging) period of $\tau / 2$, followed by a discharging (charging) period. We require that $s_{mit} + q \frac{\tau}{2} \frac{1}{E}$ does not exceed these bounds.
> 
> \begin{align*}
> q^\text{REGMAX}_{mit} = \min\Big(&q^\text{MAX}_i, \\
> & (s^\text{MAX}_{it} - s_{mit})\frac{2 C_i}{\tau} \\
> & (s_{mit} - s^\text{MIN}_{it})\frac{2 C_i}{\tau}\Big)
> \end{align*}
> for an EV capacity of $C_i$.
> 
> The $h(\cdot)$ function is linear in $r_t$ as long as charging and discharging required by the grid is possible given the SOC and management charge decisions of all of the EVs. 
> 
> We define the core valuation functions $g(\cdot)$ and $h(\cdot)$ as follows:
> \begin{align*}
>     g_i(s_{mit}, q_{mit}) =& -c_t q_{mit} \Delta t / E \,\left[q_{mit} > 0\right] + & \;\text{(charging cost)} \\
>     &-c_t q_{mit} \Delta t \,\left[q_{mit} < 0\right] + & \;\text{(discharge net-metering revenue)} \\
>     &-p^\text{OOB}_t (s^\text{MIN}_{it} - s_{mit}) \nicefrac{b_i}{\Delta t} \,\left[s^\text{MIN}_{it} < s_{mit}\right] + & \;\text{(minimum SOC penalty)} \\
>     &-p^\text{OOB}_t (s_{mit} - s^\text{MAX}_{it}) \nicefrac{b_i}{\Delta t} \,\left[s_{mit} > s^\text{MAX}_{it}\right] + & \;\text{(overcharge penalty)} \\
>     &-p^\text{PRF}_t |s_{mit} - \bar{s}_{it}| & \;\text{(SOC preference)} \\
>     h\left(r_t, \{q^\text{REGMAX}_{mit}\}, \{q_{mit}\} \right) =&p^\text{DEM} \max_t{\left(r_t + \sum_{i | q_{mit} > 0} q_{mit}\right)} + & \;\text{(demand charges)} \\
>     &b_t r_t \Delta t \,\left[r_t \le \sum_{i \in M_{mt}} |q^\text{REGMAX}_{mit} - q_{mit}|\right] + & \;\text{(regulation services)} \\
>     &-p^\text{DEF} r_t \Delta t \,\left[r_t > \sum_{i \in M_{mt}} |q^\text{REGMAX}_{mit} - q_{mit}|\right] & \;\text{(default penalty)} \\
> \end{align*}
> 
> where energy retail prices are $c_t$, and vary by season and time of day (\SIrange{0.07242}{0.1720}{\$ / kW}); $E$ is the battery charging efficiency (\num{0.95}); $p^\text{DEM}$ is peak demand charges, based on 15 minute peak charging in a given month (\SI{18.73}{\$ / kW}).
> ```
