> \subsubsection{Energy arbitrage}
> 
> The central Bellman equation for energy arbitrage is
> \[
> V_t\left(e, f_s, f_d\right) = \max_q \left[\mathbb{E}_i \left[V_{t+1}\left(T_{it}\left(e, f_s + q, f_d\right)\right)\right] + w_q\left(c_t, q\right) + w_e\left(f_s\right)\right]
> \]
> where
> \begin{itemize}
>     \item $V_t\left(e, f_s, f_d\right)$ is the value of being in state $(e, f_s, f_d)$ in time $t$. $e$ is the number of parked EVs ($e = \sum_i [d = 0]$).
>     \item $q$ is the amount of charging (+) or discharging (-).
>     \item $i$ indexes Monte Carlo simulation steps.
>     \item $T_{it}(\cdot)$ is a given transition simulation step.
>     \item $w_q\left(c_t, q\right)$ is the value of charging or discharging, given the retail price and efficiency.
>     \item $w_e\left(e, f_s\right)$ is the value of being at a given SOC to the customer.
> \end{itemize}
> 
> We define $w_q\left(c_t, q\right)$ in two pieces. The charging and discharging cost is defined in \lstinline{value_power_action}, and is applies the efficiency loss for charging:
> \[
> w_{q,1}\left(c_t, q\right) = \begin{cases}
>     -c_t K q / E & \text{if $q > 0$} \\
>     -c_t K q & \text{otherwise}
> \end{cases}
> \]
> where $K$ is the vehicle capacity times the number of plugged in vehicles, translating from SOC changes to energy changes; and $E$ is the battery efficiency, currently set to 95\%. This embeds the assumption that discharged power is sold at the retail price.
> 
> Second, we impose a charging cost on the portion of the EV distribution below the minimum SOC threshold. This is $-c_t K (f_{s,\text{min}} - f_{s,\text{below}}) P / E$, where $P$ is the portion of the plugged-in EVs below the threshold.
> 
> We define $w_e\left(e, f_s\right)$ as a function of the current SOC.
> \[
> w_e\left(f_s\right) = \begin{cases}
>     -\infty & \text{if $f_s < f_{s,\text{min}}$} \\
>     e \left(W_\text{above} (1 - P) \left(\frac{f_s - f_{s,\text{min}}}{f_{s,\text{max}} - f_{s,\text{min}}}\right)^R - W_\text{below} P\right) & \text{otherwise}
> \end{cases}
> \]
> where $W_\text{above}$ and $W_\text{above}$ weight the per-timestep valuation of SOC, and $R$ controls the elasticity of valuation with respect to the SOC. $f_{s,\text{max}}$ is set to 95\%, so that it is not incentivized to drive SOC above levels that will be healthy for the battery.
