> ```latex
> \subsubsection{Dynamic program for energy arbitrage}
> 
> For computation, we summarize the managed fleet at time $t$ by an aggregate state
> \[
> x_t = (e_t, f_{s,t}, f_{d,t}),
> \]
> where $e_t$ is the number of EVs connected to managed chargers, $f_{s,t}$ summarizes their SOC distribution, and $f_{d,t}$ summarizes anticipated departures or driving requirements. This is an approximation to the full individual-level state
> \[
> \{(s_{it}, d_{it})\}_{i=1}^N.
> \]
> 
> Let $\mathbf q_t = \{q_{it}\}_{i \in M_t}$ denote the vector of managed charging decisions, with feasible set $\mathcal Q_t(x_t)$. The transition
> \[
> x_{t+1} = \mathcal T_t(x_t,\mathbf q_t,\xi_{t+1})
> \]
> captures both battery dynamics and random deviations from expected owner behavior, where $\xi_{t+1}$ is a Monte Carlo innovation.
> 
> For energy arbitrage alone, the Bellman equation is
> \[
> V_t(x_t)
> =
> \max_{\mathbf q_t \in \mathcal Q_t(x_t)}
> \left\{
> \sum_{i \in M_t} g_i(s_{it},q_{it})
> +
> \mathbb E_{\xi_{t+1}}
> \left[
> V_{t+1}\!\left(
> \mathcal T_t(x_t,\mathbf q_t,\xi_{t+1})
> \right)
> \right]
> \right\},
> \]
> with terminal condition $V_{T+1}(x)=0$.
> 
> Thus, the previous charging-value function $w_q(\cdot)$ and SOC-value
> function $w_e(\cdot)$ are incorporated directly in $g_i(\cdot)$.
> In particular, energy purchase costs, discharge revenue, penalties for
> violating SOC bounds, and preferences for SOC near $\bar{s}_{it}$ are
> all represented by the corresponding terms of $g_i$.
